# Rust Coding Standards

## Unused Code Handling

- **Unused imports must be removed**, not suppressed with `#[allow(dead_code)]` or similar attributes
- When resolving compiler warnings about unused imports, delete the unused import statement
- `#[allow(dead_code)]` should only be used for struct fields, enum variants, or functions that are part of a public API or work-in-progress code that will be used in the future
- Never use `#[allow(dead_code)]` to silence warnings about unused imports - imports are easy to remove and re-add if needed

## Dead Code Attributes

- Use `#[allow(dead_code)]` sparingly and only when:
  - The code is part of a public API that external code may use
  - The code is work-in-progress and will be used soon
  - The code is intentionally kept for future use or debugging
- Prefer removing truly unused code over suppressing warnings
- Document why `#[allow(dead_code)]` is necessary if it's not immediately obvious

